WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.530
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Get Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:06.850
where we shared the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:08.170
I am Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.850
And in this episode,
we will talk

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.940
about creating websites
on Google Cloud using

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:13.690
virtual machines.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:15.992
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:24.270
Listen.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.030
One of the great things
about being an engineer

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.050
is that we get to have complete
control over so many aspects

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.640
of the things we develop.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.250
And for a lot of
you out there, this

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:37.960
extends to the websites
you develop too.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.660
Let's say you need
something more

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.010
advanced than a static site
but need more control than what

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.500
our managed services offer.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.320
To get more control
for the same power,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.900
you can use Compute Engine.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.780
It is important to note
that a well-designed web

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.580
application should scale
seamlessly as demand increases

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.900
and decreases and be able
to withstand the loss of one

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.180
or more compute resources.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.170
But a truly resilient and
scalable web application

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.330
requires planning.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.220
Let's see how to build one.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.460
When a user requests your
website in the browser,

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.030
their request ends up
on your DNS provider.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.500
In this case, we're
using Google DNS,

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.430
which is a highly-available
domain name service.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.040
The network traffic is
routed to infrastructure

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.230
running on Google Cloud.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.750
If you use your
own DNS provider,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.850
then the request will
land there first.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.080
And then the DNS provider routes
the traffic to Google Cloud.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.350
Then if the request is for
content that is cached,

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.570
it is delivered by CDN.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.520
In this architecture,
we're using

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.780
Cloud CDN, a global
network of edge locations.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.050
Requests are automatically
routed to the nearest edge

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:55.950
location.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:59.760
So content is delivered
with the best performance.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.450
You can integrate with any other
third-party CDN of your choice

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.350
as well.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.140
Static content used
by the web application

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.930
is stored on Google
Cloud Storage, which

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.140
is a highly durable storage
infrastructure designed

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.590
for mission critical and
primary data storage.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:21.150
HTTP requests are first handled
by Cloud Load Balancer, which

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.360
automatically distributes
the incoming application

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.440
traffic amongst multiple
Compute Engine instances.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.440
If you use HTTPS,
the SSL session

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.720
is terminated at
the load balancer

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:38.110
and requires at least one
signed SSL certificate.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.150
Web applications, including the
application server and the web

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.530
server, are deployed
on GCE instances.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.980
Deployment and scaling
is done automatically

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.580
and seamlessly by using
instance templates.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.760
An instance template is
a specific customized

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.390
configuration of Google
Compute Engine instances

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:04.170
that facilitates the reuse
of instance configuration

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.960
by using managed
instance groups.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.940
To learn more about
instance groups,

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:10.770
check out the next episode.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.990
To store application data,
you would use a database.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:20.080
The relational database used
in this example is Cloud SQL.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.160
Cloud SQL is a fully-managed
database service

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.770
that makes it easy to
set up, maintain, manage,

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:30.590
and administer your
relational data in cloud.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.700
It automates all your
backups, replication, patches,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:35.900
and updates.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.050
Now, that you know how the web
application architecture is

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:42.890
set up, what happens if your
website gets really popular

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:47.190
and the traffic grows from
hundreds to millions of users?

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.020
Well, we need to make sure that
our application can gracefully

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.110
handle peaks and
dips in our traffic.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:57.020
One way to increase
capacity to handle peaks

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:02.120
is to scale vertically by
adding more CPUs and memory

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.870
to the same instance.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.590
This means that
vertical scaling will

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.400
be limited based on the
capacity of a single machine

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.440
and its size.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.730
Horizontal scaling,
on the other hand,

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.079
is a better option for
high-availability applications

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:21.860
because it allows you to scale
the number of compute resources

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.530
dynamically as the
demand increases.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.950
The idea that the
application's resources

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:31.070
can increase or
decrease requires

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:35.090
that it has the means by which
you can add or remove instances

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.560
from service.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.200
But how does adding or removing
instances automatically work?

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.020
Tune in to the next
episode to find out.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.530
Today, we learned how to deploy
a web application on Google

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:51.770
Cloud using Compute Engine.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:54.350
That's all for today on
Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.680
Here's hoping you can
whip up something great.

